\title{bop2flex: Sample Size Deviations in Bayesian Optimal Phase II Designs}
\description{
Exact computations for the Bayesian optimal phase II design (BOP2) of Zhou, Lee and Yuan (2017) \doi{10.1002/sim.7338} and its extension with futility and efficacy stopping (BOP2-FE) of Xu, Hashimoto, Yimer and Takeda (2026) \doi{10.1080/10543406.2025.2558142}, for a single binary endpoint when the numbers of patients at the interim and final analyses differ from those planned. The boundaries at the actual analyses are derived from the error spending function implied by the planned design, so that the type I error rate does not exceed the nominal level for any sequence of analysis times chosen independently of the observed responses, and the planned boundaries are recovered when the trial follows its plan. Efficacy is never claimed with weaker posterior evidence than the planned efficacy cutoff requires at the actual sample size, and the BOP2 design never stops for efficacy at an interim analysis. Planned designs can be calibrated, with the power or the O'Brien-Fleming type efficacy cutoff, or specified by their tuning parameters or boundaries. The practice of evaluating the planned probability cutoffs at the actual sample sizes is implemented for comparison. All operating characteristics are computed exactly by propagating the distribution of the number of responses across analyses, without simulation.
}
